  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
